+# FBBR and FBBR-ServiceFair
+
+The userspace sender exposes two current source-derived entries:
+
+```bash
+make sender receiver
+./sender serverip=127.0.0.1 serverport=12345 cctype=fbbr \
+  onduration=10000 offduration=0 \
+  traffic_params=deterministic,num_cycles=1 \
+  lamda_conf=freq:5.0,fixed_amp_mbps:50.0
+
+./sender serverip=127.0.0.1 serverport=12345 cctype=fbbr-servicefair \
+  onduration=10000 offduration=0 \
+  traffic_params=deterministic,num_cycles=1 \
+  lamda_conf=freq:5.0,fixed_amp_mbps:50.0
+```
+
+`fbbr` uses the FBBR pacing waveform, Cruise regime controller, trusted
+baseline carry-forward, and service-consistent inflight envelope.
+`fbbr-servicefair` shares that path and adds the source algorithm's per-Cruise
+qdelay/service-rate AIMD, Regime III yield cap and service floor, and trusted
+baseline correction.
+
+Useful configuration keys are `freq`, `fixed_amp_mbps`, `amp_mode`,
+`min_rate_mbps`, and `window_periods`. The existing `fbbr-adaptive` and
+`fbbr-hybrid` commands remain available for legacy experiments.
+
+The ICC transport does not expose DQC's app-limited marker or the complete
+waveform-analysis data path. This port therefore disables the envelope until
+its local target/delivered histories cover one RTprop and uses the existing
+time-domain fallback classifier instead of DQC's full Goertzel/N01--N16
+classifier. It does not modify the native BBR2 state machine, MaxBw, or cwnd;
+the envelope is applied only when publishing the ICC send window.
+
+Trace records written to stderr:
+
+```text
+fbbr_cruise,...
+fbbr_regime,...
+fbbr_pacing,...
+fbbr_servicefair,...
+```
+
+Run `bash test_fbbr.sh single servicefair` for the single-flow smoke test, or
+`bash run_fbbr_dual.sh 30000 fbbr-servicefair` on a configured bottleneck for
+a two-flow fairness experiment.
